{"questions":[{"_i":65,"q":"Which biomarker is most specifically elevated in acute myocardial infarction?","a":["CK-MB","Myoglobin","Troponin I or T","LDH"],"ex":"Troponin I and T are cardiac-specific and the gold standard for AMI. CK-MB is less specific (also in skeletal muscle). Myoglobin rises early but is not cardiac-specific. High-sensitivity troponin detects even small infarctions.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Cardiac troponin I (cTnI) and troponin T (cTnT) are the biomarkers of choice when AMI is suspected — they are almost exclusively present in cardiac muscle cells and not in skeletal muscle.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Troponin: cardiac-specific","desc":"cTnI and cTnT are isoforms that occur only in the myocardium (other isoforms in skeletal muscle). With ischemic cell injury they leak out → detectable after 1-3 h."},{"title":"High-sensitivity assay","desc":"Hs-troponin detects concentrations 10-100× lower than conventional assays → positive earlier, smaller infarctions detectable, 0+1 h protocol possible."},{"title":"CK-MB and myoglobin","desc":"CK-MB: also present in skeletal muscle (less specific). Myoglobin: rises earliest (1-2 h) but also in skeletal muscle. Both have been replaced by hs-troponin in modern diagnostics."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hs-troponin I or T","desc":"Cardiac-specific, rises 1-3 h, peak 12-24 h. Gold standard for AMI diagnosis. NPV >99% with 0+1 h protocol."},{"label":"CK-MB","desc":"Less specific (skeletal muscle contamination). Useful for reinfarction (normalizes faster than troponin, so a new rise is detectable)."},{"label":"Myoglobin","desc":"Earliest rise (1-2 h) but not cardiac-specific — elevated with any muscle trauma. No longer used as the primary AMI marker."},{"label":"LDH","desc":"Rises late (48-72 h), not cardiac-specific. Historically used in late presentation — now replaced by troponin."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Troponin ≥URL + symptoms = AMI until proven otherwise. Start ACS protocol.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Non-cardiac causes","detail":"Always rule out with troponin elevation: PE, myocarditis, sepsis, renal failure, cardioversion — clinical context is decisive."}]},{"_i":66,"q":"A dry cough is a known adverse effect of ACE inhibitors.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. ACE inhibitors also inhibit the breakdown of bradykinin → accumulation → stimulation of the cough reflex. Occurs in 10-15% of patients. Solution: switch to an ARB (e.g. valsartan), which does not accumulate bradykinin.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"ACE inhibitors block not only angiotensin II production, but also the breakdown of bradykinin. Bradykinin accumulation stimulates the C-fiber cough reflex in the bronchi → dry irritating cough in 10-15% of patients (more common in Asian populations >30%).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"chance of dry cough","wiki_bigfact_sub":"with ACE inhibitor use; more common in women and Asian patients","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Bradykinin accumulation","desc":"ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) normally breaks down bradykinin. An ACE inhibitor blocks this → bradykinin accumulates in bronchial epithelium → C-fiber stimulation → dry, persistent cough."},{"title":"Distinguishing from heart failure cough","desc":"ACE inhibitor cough: dry, irritating, non-productive, continuously present, no correlation with dyspnea. Heart failure cough: nocturnal dyspnea, pink frothy sputum, orthopnea."},{"title":"ARB: no cough","desc":"ARBs block angiotensin II receptors but have no effect on bradykinin breakdown → ARBs do not cause bradykinin accumulation → no cough."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ACE inhibitor cough","desc":"Dry, irritating, present in 10-15% — switch to ARB (proven equivalent efficacy in HFrEF and hypertension). Cough disappears 1-4 weeks after stopping."},{"label":"Angioedema","desc":"Rarer serious adverse effect: sudden edema of tongue/lips/throat — life-threatening. Stop ACE inhibitor permanently, no switch to ARB (ARB-related angioedema also possible)."},{"label":"ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)","desc":"In HFrEF: replace ACE inhibitor with ARNI for additional mortality reduction. ARNI contains valsartan (ARB) + neprilysin inhibitor — increases NP peptides, also no bradykinin cough."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop ACE inhibitor in disabling cough or angioedema.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Switch to ARB","detail":"Valsartan, candesartan or losartan — comparable efficacy in HFrEF/hypertension, no cough."},{"naam":"Consider ARNI","detail":"In stable HFrEF: sacubitril/valsartan superior to ACE inhibitor for mortality reduction (PARADIGM-HF)."}]},{"_i":67,"q":"62-year-old man: calf pain when walking, disappears at rest, smoked for 30 years. ABI (ankle-brachial index) = 0.65. Diagnosis?","a":["Deep vein thrombosis","Peripheral arterial disease","Varicose veins","Diabetic neuropathy"],"ex":"PAD: intermittent claudication. ABI <0.9 = PAD, <0.4 = critical. Cause: atherosclerosis. Risk factors: smoking, diabetes, hypertension. Treatment: smoking cessation, walking training, antiplatelet therapy, statin.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) is atherosclerosis of the leg arteries. Intermittent claudication (pain when walking, relief with rest) is the classic symptom.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Atherosclerosis of leg arteries","desc":"Plaques narrow the superficial femoral artery (most frequent) → reduced blood flow distal to the obstruction."},{"title":"Exertion: demand > supply","desc":"Muscle work increases O₂ demand but the narrowed artery cannot increase flow → anaerobic metabolism → lactic acid accumulation → pain (claudication)."},{"title":"Rest: pain disappears","desc":"At rest, O₂ demand falls → lactic acid is cleared → pain disappears after 2-5 minutes. Characteristic of claudication (vs neurogenic: pain disappears more slowly, posture-dependent)."},{"title":"Critical ischemia","desc":"At ABI <0.4, flow is insufficient for basal tissue perfusion → rest pain, ulcers, impending gangrene. This is a vascular emergency."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"PAD (intermittent claudication)","desc":"Pain when walking, quickly disappears at rest, ABI <0.9. Atherosclerosis of the superficial femoral artery (most frequent)."},{"label":"DVT","desc":"Swelling, redness, D-dimer positive — pain is constant, no claudication pattern. ABI normal."},{"label":"Spinal stenosis (neurogenic claudication)","desc":"Pain + radiation in back and legs, improves with bending forward and stopping. ABI normal — the big distinction!"},{"label":"Diabetic neuropathy","desc":"Burning pain, worse at night, no direct relation to walking distance. ABI normal or mildly reduced."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Lifestyle: smoking cessation (risk ×4), walking training (evidence-based, comparable to PTA).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Pharmacological","detail":"antiplatelet agent (clopidogrel or aspirin) + high-dose statin + blood pressure control."},{"naam":"Critical ischemia","detail":"PTA (percutaneous transluminal angioplasty) or bypass surgery — indication with ABI <0.4 or rest pain."},{"naam":"Amputation","detail":"Last resort in non-revascularizable ischemia or life-threatening infection — always as distal as possible."}]},{"_i":68,"q":"What is the mechanism of action of aspirin as an antiplatelet agent?","a":["Blocks ADP receptor P2Y12","Irreversibly inhibits COX-1 → less thromboxane A2","Blocks glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","Activates plasminogen"],"ex":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 → no TXA2 synthesis → platelets aggregate less. Effect lasts for the platelet's lifetime (7-10 days). High dose = anti-inflammatory. Low dose (80-100 mg) = antithrombotic.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 in platelets → no thromboxane A2 synthesis → reduced platelet aggregation. Because platelets have no nucleus, they cannot restore COX-1 → effect lasts for the lifetime of the platelet (7-10 days).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Irreversible COX-1 acetylation","desc":"Aspirin covalently acetylates serine-530 of COX-1. Platelets lack a cell nucleus → cannot make new COX-1 → effect lasts 7-10 days (platelet lifespan)."},{"title":"No thromboxane A2","desc":"COX-1 makes prostaglandin H2 → thromboxane A2 (TXA2). TXA2 stimulates platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction. Without TXA2: platelets are less sticky."},{"title":"Dose-dependent effect","desc":"Low dose (80-100 mg): selective COX-1 in platelets (vascular wall COX-2 spared → prostacyclin I2 preserved). High dose: also COX-2 inhibition → anti-inflammatory, but more adverse effects."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Aspirin (COX-1 inhibitor)","desc":"Irreversible, duration 7-10 days, low dose antithrombotic. Standard in ACS and prevention of cardiovascular events."},{"label":"Clopidogrel/ticagrelor (P2Y12 inhibitor)","desc":"Blocks ADP receptor P2Y12 on platelets. Ticagrelor is reversible and faster than clopidogrel (prodrug). DAPT = aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor after stent."},{"label":"Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors","desc":"Abciximab, eptifibatide — block final common pathway of platelet aggregation. IV only, for high-risk PCI."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Aspirin 300 mg immediately when ACS is suspected (loading dose), then 80-100 mg/day lifelong.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"DAPT after PCI","detail":"Aspirin + ticagrelor/prasugrel for 12 months. Ticagrelor superior to clopidogrel (PLATO trial)."},{"naam":"Adverse effects","detail":"Gastrointestinal bleeding (consider PPI co-medication with high GI risk), aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease in asthma."}]},{"_i":69,"q":"60-year-old man with hypertension: sudden tearing pain between the shoulder blades, BP right 160/90, left 120/80. CT: double-barrel aorta. Diagnosis?","a":["STEMI","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Ruptured aortic aneurysm"],"ex":"Aortic dissection: intima tears → blood in aortic wall. Classic: sudden tearing pain + blood pressure difference between arms. Type A (ascending): emergency surgery. Type B (descending): medical with beta-blocker + blood pressure control.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Aortic dissection: tear in the intima → blood separates intima from media → false lumen. The "double-barrel aorta" on CT is pathognomonic. Type A mortality rises 1-2% per hour without surgery.","wiki_redflag":"ALWAYS rule out aortic dissection before thrombolysis in chest pain — thrombolysis in dissection is fatal (bleeding into the false lumen).","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Intimal rupture","desc":"Hypertension (most common), connective tissue disease (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos), bicuspid aortic valve or trauma → cyclic pressure stress tears open the intima."},{"title":"Propagation of the false lumen","desc":"Arterial pressure pumps blood through the tear → false lumen grows proximally and distally, may occlude branch arteries → organ ischemia."},{"title":"Blood pressure asymmetry","desc":"False lumen may occlude the left or right subclavian artery → blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg between arms. Characteristic sign."},{"title":"Stanford classification","desc":"Type A (60%): ascending aorta involved → risk of tamponade, aortic regurgitation, coronary ischemia → surgery. Type B (40%): descending only → medical unless complications."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Aortic dissection","desc":"Pain maximal right at onset ("tearing"), blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg, widened mediastinum on chest X-ray, CT: double-barrel aorta."},{"label":"STEMI","desc":"Pressing/squeezing pain, ST elevations on ECG, troponin rises, no blood pressure asymmetry. Thrombolysis is indicated."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Pleuritic pain, dyspnea, D-dimer elevated, no blood pressure asymmetry, no double barrel on CT."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Type A: immediate surgery (mortality 25-30% with surgery vs 50-80% without). Type B: IV labetalol or esmolol → systolic BP <120 mmHg.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Diagnostics","detail":"CT angiography of the aorta: fast, sensitive (>95%), distinguishes type A vs B and shows organ ischemia."},{"naam":"Pain control","detail":"IV morphine — reduces sympathetic activation and aortic wall stress."},{"naam":"Type B complications","detail":"TEVAR (endovascular stent) in ischemia, expansion (>5 mm/year) or aortic rupture."}]},{"_i":70,"q":"Amiodarone can cause both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Amiodarone contains 37% iodine. Can cause hypothyroidism (iodine inhibits thyroid hormone production, Wolff-Chaikoff effect) but also hyperthyroidism (iodine overload triggers autonomous production). Monitor TSH regularly.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Amiodarone contains 37% iodine — each 200 mg tablet releases ~75 mg free iodine (normal daily intake ~150 µg). This iodine overload can damage the thyroid through two mechanisms: inhibition (hypothyroidism) or stimulation (hyperthyroidism). TSH must be monitored every 6 months.","wiki_redflag":"Amiodarone-induced hyperthyroidism (AIH) type II (destructive thyroiditis) can arise suddenly and be hemodynamically significant — recognize it early. Thiamazole may not work — then prednisone (type II) or thyroidectomy.","wiki_bigfact_label":"iodine in amiodarone","wiki_bigfact_sub":"per 200 mg tablet ~75 mg free iodine — 500× the normal daily intake","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hypothyroidism (type 1)","desc":"Wolff-Chaikoff effect: iodine overload transiently inhibits thyroid hormone production. In a vulnerable thyroid (e.g. autoimmune), this is insufficiently reversible → persistent hypothyroidism."},{"title":"Hyperthyroidism type 1","desc":"Iodine-induced autonomous hormone production in underlying nodular thyroid disease (Jod-Basedow). Treatment: thiamazole + perchlorate (to block iodine uptake)."},{"title":"Hyperthyroidism type 2","desc":"Amiodarone directly damages thyroid follicles → destructive thyroiditis → massive hormone release. No nodular substrate. Treatment: corticosteroids, not thiamazole."},{"title":"Other adverse effects of amiodarone","desc":"Pulmonary toxicity (pneumonitis), liver toxicity (transaminase elevation), photodermatosis, corneal microdeposits, peripheral neuropathy. Blood levels help with dosing."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Amiodarone thyrotoxicosis (hyper + hypo)","desc":"Both can occur — monitor TSH every 6 months. With symptoms, measure TSH/fT4/fT3 immediately."},{"label":"Hypothyroidism","desc":"TSH elevated, fT4 reduced — treatment: levothyroxine, amiodarone does not necessarily need to be stopped."},{"label":"Hyperthyroidism type 1","desc":"TSH suppressed, fT4 elevated — thiamazole + perchlorate. Consider: stopping amiodarone."},{"label":"Hyperthyroidism type 2","desc":"TSH suppressed, fT4 elevated + clinically ill — prednisolone 40-60 mg/day. Ultrasound: no hypervascularization (vs type 1)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Amiodarone thyrotoxicosis: endocrinology consult + cardiology discussion about stopping vs continuing.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"TSH monitoring","detail":"Every 6 months TSH, fT4, liver enzymes, chest X-ray and ophthalmologic examination."},{"naam":"Dose reduction","detail":"Use the minimum effective amiodarone dose (generally 100-200 mg/day maintenance)."}]},{"_i":71,"q":"45-year-old woman: worst headache of her life, sudden onset, neck stiffness, photophobia. Head CT normal. What now?","a":["Give paracetamol and wait","Perform lumbar puncture","Request brain MRI","Start antibiotics immediately without further workup"],"ex":"Thunderclap headache + nuchal rigidity → always rule out subarachnoid hemorrhage. CT misses 2-5% of SAH in the first hours. Lumbar puncture: xanthochromia (yellow fluid) or elevated erythrocytes after 12 hours confirms SAH. Cause: aneurysm rupture.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Thunderclap headache + neck stiffness + negative CT = subarachnoid hemorrhage until proven otherwise. CT misses 5% of SAH. Lumbar puncture after ≥6 hours demonstrating xanthochromia is the mandatory next step.","wiki_redflag":""Worst headache of my life" is an absolute alarm sign. Do not miss this as migraine. A negative CT does NOT rule out SAH — always LP.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Aneurysm rupture","desc":"80% due to rupture of a saccular aneurysm at a bifurcation of the circle of Willis → blood in subarachnoid space."},{"title":"Thunderclap headache","desc":"Sudden intracranial pressure rise due to bleeding irritates dural receptors → maximum pain within seconds."},{"title":"CT sensitivity falls","desc":"CT is 98% sensitive in the first 6 h, falls to 85% after 24 h. After 6-12 h: LP for xanthochromia (breakdown product of hemoglobin → yellow discoloration)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"SAH","desc":"Thunderclap + neck stiffness + CT negative. LP: xanthochromia after 12 h or elevated erythrocytes. Aneurysm rupture until proven otherwise."},{"label":"Bacterial meningitis","desc":"Fever more prominent, slower onset (hours), PMN pleocytosis in CSF, no sudden thunderclap."},{"label":"Migraine","desc":"Gradual build-up, previous episodes, never thunderclap at first attack. Never simply label as migraine without SAH exclusion."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate CT → if CT negative: LP after ≥6-12 h for xanthochromia. Call neurosurgery/neuroradiology if confirmed.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Aneurysm treatment","detail":"Endovascular coiling (preferred) or neurosurgical clipping of the aneurysm. Within 24-72 h to prevent rerupture."},{"naam":"Vasospasm prevention","detail":"Nimodipine 60 mg orally 4x/day for 21 days — reduces ischemic risk from vasospasm days 4-14."},{"naam":"Rerupture","detail":"30% risk of rerupture in the first days without treatment — mortality with rerupture >70%."}]},{"_i":72,"q":"In ischemic stroke, thrombolysis with alteplase may be given up to 4.5 hours after symptom onset.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Alteplase thrombolysis may be given up to 4.5 hours after symptom onset (if no contraindications). Earlier = better: "time is brain". Contraindications: recent surgery, bleeding diathesis, blood pressure >185/110, blood glucose <2.7 or >22.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Alteplase (tPA) dissolves the clot occluding a cerebral artery. The time window is 4.5 hours — but the earlier, the better: every minute of delay = ~1.9 million fewer neurons.","wiki_redflag":"Thrombolysis in hemorrhagic stroke is fatal. Always CT first. Uncontrolled hypertension (>185/110) must be treated before administration.","wiki_bigfact_label":"Thrombolysis time window","wiki_bigfact_sub":"After 4.5 h: benefit falls, bleeding risk rises. Earlier = always better.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"tPA action","desc":"Alteplase activates plasminogen → plasmin → fibrinolysis of the clot → restored perfusion."},{"title":"Time window","desc":"After 4.5 h: ischemic tissue is partly already infarcting → reperfusion injury + hemorrhagic transformation outweigh the benefit."},{"title":"Contraindications","desc":"Recent surgery, bleeding diathesis, BP >185/110, glucose <2.7 or >22, active oral anticoagulants."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Alteplase ≤4.5 h","desc":"Indicated in ischemic stroke, if no contraindications. Dose 0.9 mg/kg IV."},{"label":"Tenecteplase","desc":"Alternative in trials — comparable efficacy, single bolus. Increasingly used."},{"label":"Thrombectomy","desc":"For large-vessel occlusion (M1, ICA): mechanical thrombectomy up to 24 h — superior in proximal occlusion."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate CT (rule out hemorrhage) → alteplase 0.9 mg/kg IV (10% bolus, rest over 60 min) ≤4.5 h.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Treat BP","detail":"BP >185/110: labetalol or nicardipine IV. Thrombolysis is safe only after BP control."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"ICU 24 h after thrombolysis: neurological status hourly, BP control, delay aspirin for 24 h."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"Aspirin + high-dose statin after 24 h in ischemic stroke. Anticoagulants for AF after 2-14 days."}]},{"_i":73,"q":"25-year-old man: tonic-clonic seizure for 3 minutes, then confused and sleepy. No fever. First seizure. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Meningitis","Epilepsy","Hypoglycemia","Psychogenic seizure"],"ex":"Epilepsy: tonic-clonic seizure + postictal phase (confusion, fatigue). First seizure: EEG and MRI for structural cause. Antiepileptic drug after two seizures or increased recurrence risk. Driving ban applies until seizure-free for 1 year.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"A generalized tonic-clonic seizure with postictal phase (confusion, fatigue, sleep) is the classic picture of epilepsy. After a first seizure: EEG + MRI to determine structural cause and seizure type.","wiki_redflag":"Persistent seizure >5 minutes = status epilepticus. First seizure in an adult: always MRI to rule out tumor, abscess, hemorrhage or dysplasia.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hyperexcitability","desc":"Uncontrolled neuronal discharge — too much excitation (glutamate) or too little inhibition (GABA) → spread through both hemispheres."},{"title":"Tonic phase","desc":"Massive motor discharge → muscle stiffness, apnea, cyanosis (seconds to 1 minute)."},{"title":"Clonic phase","desc":"Alternating inhibition → rhythmic muscle jerks. Then postictal exhaustion (minutes to hours)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Epilepsy","desc":"Tonic-clonic + postictal phase + EEG abnormality. Investigate cause: MRI, blood tests (glucose, electrolytes, Na)."},{"label":"Syncope","desc":"Brief loss of consciousness, a few muscle jerks possible but no tongue bite or prolonged postictal state. EEG normal."},{"label":"Hypoglycemia","desc":"Glucose <2.8 → seizure. Always measure blood glucose in a seizure! Correctable."},{"label":"Meningitis","desc":"Fever + neck stiffness + photophobia. Seizures possible but infectious picture predominates."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Seizure >5 min: diazepam 10 mg rectally or midazolam 10 mg buccally.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"First seizure","detail":"Not always treated immediately — recurrence risk after first seizure is 40-50%. Decision depends on EEG, MRI and risk profile."},{"naam":"Antiepileptic drug","detail":"After second seizure or high recurrence risk: levetiracetam or lamotrigine (broad spectrum). Valproate for generalized epilepsy."},{"naam":"Driving ban","detail":"At least 1 year seizure-free before driving. Patient must know this — reporting obligation in NL."}]},{"_i":74,"q":"30-year-old woman: episodic bilateral arm and leg weakness, drooping eyelid (ptosis), worse at end of day. Improvement after ice on eye. Diagnosis?","a":["Multiple sclerosis","Myasthenia gravis","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Guillain-Barré"],"ex":"MG: autoantibodies against acetylcholine receptor → impaired neuromuscular transmission. Classic: fatigable weakness, ptosis, fluctuating course. Ice test: cold temporarily improves transmission. Treatment: pyridostigmine, immunosuppression, thymectomy.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies block the neuromuscular junction. Key feature: fatigable muscle weakness — worse with use, better after rest. This explains the "end-of-day" worsening and the ice test.","wiki_redflag":"Myasthenic crisis: worsening of respiratory muscles → life-threatening respiratory insufficiency. Monitor FVC — if it falls: ICU admission and IVIG/plasmapheresis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"AChR antibodies","desc":"Autoantibodies bind acetylcholine receptors (AChR) at the motor endplate → fewer receptors → muscle fibers respond less and less."},{"title":"Ice test","desc":"Cold temporarily improves acetylcholine release and inhibits antibody activity → ptosis improves with 2 min of ice on the eye — diagnostically useful."},{"title":"Thymus pathology","desc":"70% have thymic hyperplasia, 10-15% thymoma. Thymectomy gives remission in young patients <60 years."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Myasthenia gravis","desc":"Fatigable weakness (worse in the evening), ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria. AChR antibodies positive in 85%. Ice test positive."},{"label":"Lambert-Eaton (LEMS)","desc":"Also neuromuscular but weakness actually improves with repeated use (presynaptic defect). Paraneoplastic in lung cancer (VGCC antibodies)."},{"label":"MS","desc":"CNS demyelination, sensation affected, MRI lesions. Weakness not related to fatigability."},{"label":"ALS","desc":"Motor but irreversible, fasciculations, atrophy, no extraocular muscles."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Pyridostigmine (anticholinesterase) — increases amount of ACh at the synapse. Rapidly effective, symptomatic.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Immunosuppression","detail":"Prednisolone + azathioprine for long-term disease-modifying treatment."},{"naam":"Thymectomy","detail":"Always for thymoma; also effective in ≤60 years without thymoma (MGTX trial)."},{"naam":"Crisis","detail":"IVIG 2 g/kg or plasmapheresis — rapidly effective with respiratory threat."},{"naam":"Forbidden medication","detail":"Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, chloroquine — worsen neuromuscular transmission."}]},{"_i":75,"q":"Multiple sclerosis affects both the central and peripheral nervous systems.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. MS is a demyelinating disease of the central nervous system (brain + spinal cord). The peripheral nervous system is spared. Key feature: lesions separated in time and space. Diagnosis: MRI + clinical picture + CSF examination.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"MS is an autoimmune demyelinating disease that affects only the central nervous system (brain, spinal cord, optic nerve). The peripheral nervous system is spared — this is a crucial distinguishing feature.","wiki_redflag":"MS is not a peripheral neuropathy! GBS, CIDP and polyneuropathy affect the PNS. MS: lesions in the white matter of the CNS, disseminated in time and space.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Autoimmune demyelination of CNS","desc":"T cells infiltrate the CNS, damage myelin of axons → conduction delay or block → neurological deficit."},{"title":"Disseminated in time and space","desc":"McDonald criteria require ≥2 attacks in ≥2 CNS locations. MRI can confirm "dissemination in space and time" without a second attack."},{"title":"PNS spared","desc":"Schwann cells (PNS myelin) are not affected. Reflexes normal or increased (UMN features)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"MS (CNS)","desc":"CNS demyelination: periventricular MRI lesions, oligoclonal bands, optic neuritis. PNS intact."},{"label":"GBS (PNS)","desc":"Ascending paralysis + areflexia — PNS demyelination. CSF: albuminocytologic dissociation."},{"label":"NMO (Devic)","desc":"Also CNS but specifically optic nerve + spinal cord, AQP4 antibodies, more severe attacks than MS."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute relapse: methylprednisolone 1 g IV for 3-5 days (accelerates recovery, no effect on long-term course).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Disease-modifying therapy","detail":"Interferon-beta, glatiramer acetate (mild MS) or natalizumab/ocrelizumab (active MS) — reduce relapse frequency."},{"naam":"Diagnosis","detail":"McDonald criteria: MRI (periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, spinal) + CSF (oligoclonal bands) + clinical picture."}]},{"_i":76,"q":"65-year-old man: progressive memory loss, personality change, symptoms for 3 years. MRI: diffuse temporoparietal cortical atrophy. Diagnosis?","a":["Vascular dementia","Alzheimer's disease","Lewy body dementia","Frontotemporal dementia"],"ex":"Alzheimer's disease: most common form of dementia (60-70%). Pathology: amyloid plaques + tau neurofibrillary tangles. Temporoparietal atrophy on MRI. Early: memory problems. Later: apraxia, agnosia. Treatment: cholinesterase inhibitors (symptomatic).","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Alzheimer's disease is the most common form of dementia (60-70%). It starts insidiously with memory loss for recent events, progressive over years to complete care dependency. MRI: temporoparietal atrophy.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"Of all dementia","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Alzheimer's is by far the most common cause. Prevalence doubles every 5 years after 65.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Amyloid plaques","desc":"Beta-amyloid accumulates extracellularly between neurons → inflammation + synaptic dysfunction."},{"title":"Tau neurofibrillary tangles","desc":"Hyperphosphorylated tau protein clumps intracellularly → axonal transport disrupted → neuronal death."},{"title":"Cholinergic deficit","desc":"Nucleus basalis of Meynert (ACh-producing) degenerates early → memory and attention decline. Hence cholinesterase inhibitors."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Alzheimer's disease","desc":"Insidious onset, episodic memory affected first, MRI temporoparietal atrophy, amyloid PET or CSF biomarkers positive."},{"label":"Lewy body dementia (DLB)","desc":"Visual hallucinations + parkinsonism + REM sleep behavior disorder. CAVEAT: antipsychotics are dangerous (severe hypersensitivity reactions)."},{"label":"Vascular dementia","desc":"Stepwise course, cardiovascular risk factors, white matter abnormalities on MRI, lacunar infarcts."},{"label":"Frontotemporal dementia (FTD)","desc":"Young onset (<65 years), personality change or language deficit before memory symptoms. Frontal atrophy on MRI."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No curative treatment — cholinesterase inhibitors for symptom delay.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Cholinesterase inhibitors","detail":"Donepezil or rivastigmine — increase acetylcholine, modest symptom improvement in mild-moderate Alzheimer's."},{"naam":"Memantine","detail":"NMDA receptor antagonist — in moderate-severe Alzheimer's, can be combined with cholinesterase inhibitor."},{"naam":"Anti-amyloid antibodies","detail":"Lecanemab and donanemab: slow clinical decline in early Alzheimer's (recently approved)."}]},{"_i":77,"q":"35-year-old woman: sudden unilateral vision loss, pain with eye movements, color appears less bright. MRI: white matter abnormality in optic nerve. Diagnosis?","a":["Glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Migraine with aura"],"ex":"Optic neuritis: inflammation of the optic nerve, often the first manifestation of MS. Pain with eye movements + unilateral vision loss + reduced color perception. Treatment: IV methylprednisolone accelerates recovery. Brain MRI mandatory.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Optic neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve, in 50% of cases the first manifestation of MS. The classic triad: acute unilateral vision loss + pain with eye movements + reduced color perception (red looks gray).","wiki_redflag":"After optic neuritis: 50% chance of MS diagnosis within 15 years. Brain MRI is mandatory — lesions present → high conversion risk → consider starting disease-modifying therapy.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Autoimmune demyelination","desc":"Inflammatory cells damage myelin of the optic nerve → slowed or blocked impulse conduction → vision loss and pain."},{"title":"Pain mechanism","desc":"The optic nerve runs through a confined space → swelling with inflammation irritates the nerve sheath → pain with eye movements (traction)."},{"title":"Color vision","desc":"Papillomacular bundle (color vision, central vision) is most sensitive to demyelination."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Optic neuritis (MS)","desc":"Young, female, pain with eye movements, unilateral, reduced color vision. MRI: optic nerve lesion. VEP delayed."},{"label":"NMO (Devic)","desc":"More severe optic neuritis + longitudinal myelitis. AQP4 antibodies positive. Poor prognosis, different treatment."},{"label":"Glaucoma","desc":"Chronic, painless, elevated intraocular pressure, peripheral visual field loss. No pain with eye movements."},{"label":"Retinal detachment","desc":"Sudden painless vision loss + "curtain" or flashes. No pain with movement. Ophthalmologic emergency."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Methylprednisolone 1 g IV for 3 days — accelerates recovery, no effect on final outcome.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Brain MRI","detail":"Mandatory after every optic neuritis — lesions present: increased MS conversion risk → discuss preventive treatment."},{"naam":"VEP","detail":"Visual evoked potential: delayed P100 wave confirms optic damage even after recovery of visual acuity."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"90% recover visually after 3-6 months. Color perception and contrast sensitivity may remain permanently impaired."}]},{"_i":78,"q":"Which medication is first choice in generalized tonic-clonic epilepsy?","a":["Carbamazepine","Sodium valproate","Phenytoin","Gabapentin"],"ex":"Valproate is a broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug: works in generalized and partial seizures. Inhibits Na channels and increases GABA. Caveat: teratogenic — absolutely avoid in women of childbearing potential without adequate contraception. Alternative: levetiracetam.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Sodium valproate is the broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug of first choice in generalized epilepsy. It works through multiple mechanisms (Na channel inhibition, GABA increase) but is strongly teratogenic — causes spina bifida and cognitive damage in the child.","wiki_redflag":"Valproate is ABSOLUTELY contraindicated in pregnant women and women of childbearing potential without effective contraception. Alternative: levetiracetam or lamotrigine.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Na channel inhibition","desc":"Inhibits rapid repetitive neuronal firing → lowers seizure threshold."},{"title":"GABA increase","desc":"Inhibits GABA transaminase → more inhibitory GABA activity in CNS."},{"title":"Teratogenicity","desc":"Valproate inhibits folate metabolism and epigenetic processes → neural tube defects (spina bifida 2-3%), dysmorphisms, lower IQ in child."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Valproate (broad-spectrum)","desc":"Tonic-clonic + absence + myoclonus. First choice in men and women without pregnancy wish."},{"label":"Levetiracetam","desc":"Broad-spectrum, safer profile, no teratogenicity worry — alternative in women of childbearing potential."},{"label":"Carbamazepine","desc":"Narrow spectrum — focal epilepsy only. Can worsen generalized epilepsy (absence, myoclonus)."},{"label":"Ethosuximide","desc":"Only for absence epilepsy — no effect on tonic-clonic seizures."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Status epilepticus: benzodiazepine IV/rectal → phenytoin or levetiracetam IV.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Start valproate","detail":"500-1000 mg/day, slowly increase. Therapeutic: serum levels 50-100 mg/L."},{"naam":"Women of childbearing potential","detail":"Levetiracetam or lamotrigine as first choice — valproate only if alternatives fail + good contraception."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Liver enzymes, ammonia (with confusion), weight (valproate → weight gain)."}]},{"_i":79,"q":"55-year-old man: progressive weakness in legs, later arms, fasciculations, no sensory disturbances, hyperreflexia. Diagnosis?","a":["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Multiple sclerosis","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Myasthenia gravis"],"ex":"ALS: degeneration of central and peripheral motor neurons. Combination of UMN (hyperreflexia, spasticity) + LMN (fasciculations, atrophy) without sensory loss. Progressive, fatal on average in 3-5 years. Riluzole slightly prolongs survival.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"ALS (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis) is progressive degeneration of both the upper (UMN) and lower (LMN) motor neuron. Key features: muscle weakness + fasciculations + hyperreflexia — but never sensory loss.","wiki_redflag":"ALS completely spares sensation. Sensory loss with a similar picture: think of MS, compression or GBS. Respiratory insufficiency is the most common cause of death.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"UMN degeneration","desc":"Motor cortex → hyperreflexia, spasticity, Babinski sign, pathological reflexes."},{"title":"LMN degeneration","desc":"Anterior horn cell in spinal cord/brainstem → muscle atrophy, fasciculations, hypotonia, loss of strength."},{"title":"Sensation spared","desc":"Sensory tracts in the spinal cord remain intact — crucial distinguishing feature of ALS."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ALS","desc":"UMN + LMN simultaneously, no sensory loss. Progressively fatal. EMG: denervation + fasciculations."},{"label":"MS","desc":"CNS demyelination, relapsing-remitting, sensory disturbances present, MRI lesions."},{"label":"GBS","desc":"Ascending paralysis + areflexia + sensory deficit. Acute onset, after infection."},{"label":"Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA)","desc":"LMN only, no UMN features. Genetic (SMN1 mutation). Slower course."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No curative treatment — supportive and life-prolonging care.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Riluzole","detail":"Glutamate antagonist — prolongs survival by an average of 3-6 months. Only registered pharmacological therapy."},{"naam":"Edaravone (Radicava)","detail":"Free radical scavenger — slows functional decline in early ALS in selected patients."},{"naam":"Symptomatic","detail":"Sialorrhea: glycopyrrolate. Dysphagia: PEG tube. Respiratory insufficiency: NIV (non-invasive ventilation)."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"Median survival 3-5 years after diagnosis. 10% survive >10 years (including Stephen Hawking as an exception)."}]},{"_i":80,"q":"Guillain-Barré syndrome typically begins in the arms and spreads to the legs.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. GBS typically begins in the legs (ascending paralysis) and spreads upward. Cause: autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerves, often after infection (Campylobacter, CMV). Treatment: IVIG or plasmapheresis. Watch respiration.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"GBS begins in the legs and ascends upward (ascending paralysis) — the opposite of the statement in the question. The danger is respiratory muscle failure when the deficit reaches the thorax.","wiki_redflag":"30% of GBS patients require ventilation. Monitor vital capacity (FVC) every 4-6 h. FVC <20 ml/kg or rapidly falling → elective intubation before crisis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Molecular mimicry","desc":"Antibodies against Campylobacter, CMV or EBV cross-react with gangliosides on peripheral myelin → ascending demyelination."},{"title":"Ascending pattern","desc":"Distal legs first → proximal legs → trunk → arms → cranial nerves (facial palsy, dysphagia)."},{"title":"Areflexia","desc":"Loss of myelin in sensory arcs → areflexia is a characteristic early sign, even with still minimal weakness."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"GBS","desc":"Ascending paralysis + areflexia + albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF. Acute, after infection."},{"label":"ALS","desc":"Chronic-progressive, UMN + LMN together, no sensory loss, fasciculations."},{"label":"Botulism","desc":"Descending paralysis from top to bottom. Abnormal pupillary responses. No fever."},{"label":"Transverse myelitis","desc":"Spinal level, bladder/bowel involved early, UMN features (hyperreflexia)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IVIG 2 g/kg over 5 days OR plasmapheresis — equally effective. No corticosteroids.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"FVC monitoring","detail":"Vital capacity every 4-6 h. FVC <20 ml/kg = indication for intubation. The "20-30-40 rule": FVC <20, PaO2 <30 mmHg, PaCO2 >40 → ICU."},{"naam":"Pain treatment","detail":"Dysesthesia/nerve pain: gabapentin or amitriptyline. NSAIDs poorly effective."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"80% complete recovery after 6-12 months. 5% remain severely disabled."}]},{"_i":81,"q":"72-year-old man: resting tremor of right hand, stiffness, slowness, small steps. Diagnosis?","a":["Essential tremor","Parkinson's disease","Multiple system atrophy","Huntington's disease"],"ex":"Parkinson's disease: loss of dopaminergic neurons in substantia nigra. Classic triad: resting tremor (pill-rolling movements) + rigidity + bradykinesia. Asymmetric onset. Treatment: levodopa (most effective). Non-motor symptoms: depression, smell disturbances.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Parkinson's disease is caused by progressive loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra. When 70-80% are lost, the classic triad appears: resting tremor + rigidity + bradykinesia — always asymmetric onset.","wiki_redflag":"Parkinson-plus syndromes (MSA, PSP, CBS) respond poorly to levodopa and have a more severe prognosis. Early falls, symmetric onset and poor levodopa response are alarm signs.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Alpha-synuclein","desc":"Protein clumps together in Lewy bodies → accumulates in neurons of substantia nigra → accelerated cell death."},{"title":"Prodromal phase","desc":"Smell disturbance, REM sleep behavior disorder and constipation precede motor symptoms by years — the disease begins outside the brain."},{"title":"Dopamine deficiency","desc":"Striatum receives too little dopamine → basal ganglia circuit disrupted → movement initiation inhibited → bradykinesia + rigidity + tremor."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Parkinson's disease","desc":"Resting tremor (4-6 Hz, pill-rolling), asymmetric onset, bradykinesia, rigidity, good levodopa response, no early falls."},{"label":"Essential tremor","desc":"Postural tremor (not at rest), bilateral, improves with alcohol, no bradykinesia/rigidity. Family history."},{"label":"Parkinson-plus (MSA/PSP)","desc":"Early autonomic dysfunction (MSA) or early backward falls (PSP). Poor levodopa response. More severe than idiopathic Parkinson's."},{"label":"DLB (Lewy body dementia)","desc":"Parkinsonism + dementia + visual hallucinations simultaneously. CAVEAT: antipsychotics are dangerous."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Levodopa + carbidopa (dopa decarboxylase inhibitor) — most effective, gold standard.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Dopamine agonists","detail":"Pramipexole or ropinirole as alternative in younger patients (<70 years) — less "wearing off", more psychiatric adverse effects."},{"naam":"MAO-B inhibitors","detail":"Rasagiline or selegiline — mild efficacy, neuroprotective effect unconfirmed."},{"naam":"Deep brain stimulation (DBS)","detail":"For disabling tremor or motor fluctuations after medication optimization — subthalamic nucleus as target."},{"naam":"Non-motor","detail":"Antidepressants (depression in 40%), melatonin (sleep disorder), laxatives, physiotherapy."}]},{"_i":82,"q":"40-year-old woman: migraine attacks, now also weakness of left arm during attack. What is the term for this?","a":["Aura without headache","Hemiplegic migraine","Basilar migraine","TIA"],"ex":"Hemiplegic migraine: rare form in which motor deficit occurs as an aura symptom. Important: first rule out TIA/stroke! Familial form: CACNA1A gene mutation. Triptans and ergotamines contraindicated in hemiplegic migraine.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Hemiplegic migraine is a rare migraine variant in which motor deficit (hemiparesis) occurs as an aura symptom — fully reversible. It is not a stroke, but TIA/stroke MUST always be ruled out in a first episode.","wiki_redflag":"Triptans and ergotamines are CONTRAINDICATED in hemiplegic migraine — risk of vasospasm and ischemic stroke. Use paracetamol/NSAID for attack treatment.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cortical spreading depression","desc":"Waves of neuronal hyperactivity followed by depression spread across the cortex → motor cortex → temporary hemiparesis as aura."},{"title":"Familial form (FHM)","desc":"Autosomal dominant. Mutations in ion channels (CACNA1A, ATP1A2, SCN1A) disrupt ion homeostasis → increased sensitivity to spreading depression."},{"title":"Reversibility","desc":"The deficit resolves completely after minutes to hours — this is the characteristic difference from a stroke."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hemiplegic migraine","desc":"Motor aura + headache. Fully reversible. Previous migraine episodes. CACNA1A mutation possible."},{"label":"TIA","desc":"No preceding headache, age >50 years, cardiovascular risk factors. Always rule out in first episode."},{"label":"Basilar migraine","desc":"Aura with brainstem symptoms (dysarthria, diplopia, ataxia, bilateral tingling) — no hemiparesis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"NO triptans or ergotamines. Paracetamol 1000 mg or NSAID for attack treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Attack treatment","detail":"Paracetamol, naproxen or ibuprofen. Domperidone for nausea. No triptans."},{"naam":"Prophylaxis","detail":"Valproate or topiramate — for frequent or disabling attacks."},{"naam":"Genetic counseling","detail":"In familial hemiplegic migraine: consider CACNA1A gene test. Inform family members."}]},{"_i":83,"q":"CSF: elevated protein, normal glucose, lymphocytosis, oligoclonal bands present. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Bacterial meningitis","Multiple sclerosis","Viral meningitis","Subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"ex":"Oligoclonal bands in CSF (not in serum) are characteristic of MS — present in >95%. Viral meningitis: lymphocytosis + normal protein + normal/slightly reduced glucose. Bacterial: neutrophilic pleocytosis + markedly elevated protein + low glucose.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Oligoclonal bands (OCB) in CSF but not in serum are most characteristic of MS — present in >95% of MS patients. They reflect local immunoglobulin production in the CNS.","wiki_redflag":"Oligoclonal bands are not 100% specific for MS — also in neuroborreliosis, neurosyphilis and NMOSD. Always combine with MRI and clinical picture for diagnosis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"IgG production in CNS","desc":"In MS, activated B cells in the CNS produce clonal IgG antibodies → visible as sharp bands on isoelectric focusing of CSF."},{"title":"CSF vs serum","desc":"OCB only in CSF (not serum) = intrathecal production. OCB in both: systemic infection/inflammation."},{"title":"Glucose normal","desc":"In MS, glucose is normal — low glucose points to bacterial meningitis (bacteria consume glucose)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"MS","desc":"OCB in CSF (not serum), mild lymphocytosis, slightly elevated protein, normal glucose. >95% sensitivity."},{"label":"Bacterial meningitis","desc":"Neutrophilic pleocytosis (>1000 cells), markedly elevated protein, low glucose (<60% serum glucose), Gram stain positive."},{"label":"Viral meningitis","desc":"Mild-moderate lymphocytosis, normal or slightly elevated protein, normal glucose. No OCB."},{"label":"SAH","desc":"Bloody CSF, xanthochromia after centrifugation. No OCB. Normal cell count (erythrocytes)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No emergency indication with MS CSF profile — establish diagnosis and discuss disease-modifying therapy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"McDonald criteria","detail":"OCB in CSF can replace "dissemination in time" in McDonald criteria — accelerates MS diagnosis after first attack."},{"naam":"Disease-modifying therapy","detail":"After MS diagnosis: interferon-beta, glatiramer acetate or natalizumab/ocrelizumab depending on activity."}]},{"_i":84,"q":"In bacterial meningitis, lumbar puncture must always be delayed until after CT scan.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Antibiotics must NOT be delayed for CT. Start antibiotics immediately if there are signs of increased intracranial pressure (papilledema, decreased consciousness) and then do CT. Otherwise: LP first. Delaying antibiotics significantly increases mortality.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"In bacterial meningitis, starting antibiotics always comes before CT. Delaying antibiotics for CT significantly increases mortality. LP can wait; antibiotics cannot.","wiki_redflag":"Every hour of antibiotic delay in bacterial meningitis increases the chance of death or permanent damage. Start ceftriaxone + dexamethasone as soon as the diagnosis is considered.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Increased intracranial pressure","desc":"Meningitis → inflammation → edema → increased ICP. LP with increased ICP → risk of herniation (transtentorial or uncal)."},{"title":"Indications for CT before LP","desc":"Signs of increased ICP: papilledema, focal deficit, decreased consciousness, immunocompromised. Then: CT first, antibiotics immediately without waiting for LP."},{"title":"Antibiotics before LP","desc":"Starting antibiotics changes the CSF culture but not the meningitis diagnosis — clinical picture and PCR remain useful."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"No signs of increased pressure","desc":"LP immediately → antibiotics after CSF sampling. No CT required first."},{"label":"Increased pressure (papilledema/unconsciousness)","desc":"Antibiotics + dexamethasone immediately → CT → LP if safe. No antibiotic delay."},{"label":"Petechiae + septic shock","desc":"Meningococcal sepsis: antibiotics already in ambulance. LP later when stable."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Ceftriaxone 2 g IV + dexamethasone 10 mg IV — immediately, even before CT or LP if increased pressure is suspected.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Dexamethasone","detail":"10 mg IV 4x/day for 4 days — lowers mortality in pneumococcal meningitis. Start simultaneously with antibiotics."},{"naam":"LP timing","detail":"LP immediately if no increased ICP signs. CT first with focal deficit, decreased consciousness, papilledema or immunosuppression."},{"naam":"Empiric coverage","detail":"Ceftriaxone covers S. pneumoniae + N. meningitidis. Add amoxicillin if >50 years (Listeria)."}]},{"_i":85,"q":"8-year-old child: sudden loss of awareness, staring gaze for 10 seconds, then normal. No tonic-clonic movements. EEG: 3 Hz spike-wave. Diagnosis?","a":["Grand mal seizure (tonic-clonic)","Absence epilepsy","Focal epilepsy","Syncope"],"ex":"Absence epilepsy: brief interruption of awareness, no postictal phase, typical 3 Hz generalized spike-wave on EEG. Begins in childhood, often spontaneous remission. Treatment: ethosuximide or valproate. No driving ban until 1 year free.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Absence epilepsy is a generalized epilepsy variant in children: brief (5-20 sec) interruption of awareness with staring gaze, no postictal phase. EEG: 3 Hz generalized spike-wave is pathognomonic.","wiki_redflag":"Absence epilepsy can be confused with daydreaming or attention problems — the diagnosis is often missed. Dozens of absences per day = major learning impact. Hyperventilation provokes absences during EEG.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"3 Hz spike-wave","desc":"Generalized oscillation between thalamus and cortex at 3 Hz → bilateral interruption of awareness, no motor component."},{"title":"Short duration","desc":"5-20 seconds, then immediately normal — no postictal phase (distinguishes from tonic-clonic!)."},{"title":"Hyperventilation provocation","desc":"Hyperventilating for 3 minutes induces absences during EEG — diagnostically useful."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Absence epilepsy","desc":"Child, staring gaze, <20 sec, no postictal phase, 3 Hz spike-wave EEG. Sometimes lip smacking or blinking."},{"label":"Tonic-clonic seizure","desc":"Longer (>2 min), motor components, postictal confusion/fatigue. Different EEG pattern."},{"label":"Focal epilepsy","desc":"Awareness may be impaired but with focal motor component or automatisms. EEG: focal discharge."},{"label":"Syncope","desc":"Brief, triggered by standing/heat/emotion. EEG normal. Normal or slow heart rate."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No acute intervention needed — absences are brief and self-limiting.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Ethosuximide","detail":"First choice in pure absence epilepsy — 80% effective, effect only on absences (not tonic-clonic)."},{"naam":"Valproate","detail":"Alternative — broad-spectrum, effective if tonic-clonic seizures also occur. Caveat: teratogenic in girls."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"70% spontaneous remission in adolescence. Sometimes evolution to other epilepsy forms."}]},{"_i":86,"q":"50-year-old man: acute unilateral facial paralysis, forehead also affected, no other deficits. Diagnosis?","a":["Central stroke","Bell's palsy (peripheral facial nerve palsy)","TIA","Brain tumor"],"ex":"Bell's palsy: peripheral facial nerve palsy → forehead also affected (central stroke: forehead spared due to bilateral innervation). Cause: often HSV reactivation. Treatment: prednisolone within 72 hours + acyclovir. Prognosis: 80% complete recovery.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Bell's palsy is an acute peripheral palsy of the facial nerve (CN VII), presumably due to HSV-1 reactivation in the geniculate ganglion.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"HSV-1 reactivation."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"inflammation + edema in the bony facial canal."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"compression."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"demyelination. Trigger factors: stress, upper respiratory infection, cold. Rare other causes: Lyme disease (Borrelia), sarcoidosis, parotid carcinoma."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bell's palsy (peripheral facial nerve palsy)","desc":"Forehead ALSO affected (CN VII branch to m. frontalis). Peripheral motor neuron lesion → all ipsilateral facial muscles paralyzed."},{"label":"Central stroke","desc":"Forehead SPARED due to bilateral cortical innervation (frontal branch has cortical input from both hemispheres). Central lesion = no forehead paralysis."},{"label":"Ramsay Hunt syndrome","desc":"VZV reactivation: Bell's palsy + painful vesicles in the ear (zoster oticus) + hearing loss. Worse prognosis, acyclovir essential."},{"label":"Parotid tumor","desc":"Slow progression over weeks-months, painless hard tumor, no tendency to recover."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day (max 80 mg) for 10 days — proven effective, start within 72 h after symptom onset.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Acyclovir","detail":"Add in severe palsy or Ramsay Hunt — antiviral effect on HSV/VZV reactivation."},{"naam":"Eye protection","detail":"Artificial tears during day + eye patch at night — lagophthalmos can cause corneal damage."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"80% complete recovery within 3 months. Electromyography after 2 weeks if incomplete recovery."}]},{"_i":87,"q":"Dopamine can cross the blood-brain barrier and is therefore given directly as medication in Parkinson's disease.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Dopamine does NOT cross the blood-brain barrier. Therefore levodopa (precursor) is given, which does cross and is converted in the brain. Levodopa is combined with carbidopa/benserazide (peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor) to reduce adverse effects.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Dopamine does not cross the blood-brain barrier — it is a charged molecule. Levodopa (L-DOPA) is the precursor that DOES cross the BBB via amino acid transporters, and is converted to dopamine in the brain.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"BBB selectivity","desc":"The blood-brain barrier allows only lipophilic or actively transported molecules through. Dopamine is too polar/hydrophilic and is not transported."},{"title":"Levodopa transport","desc":"Levodopa structurally resembles amino acids → transport via LAT1 (large neutral amino acid transporter) through the BBB."},{"title":"Carbidopa/benserazide","desc":"Peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor: prevents levodopa from already being converted to dopamine in the blood (adverse effects: nausea, hypotension). Does not cross the BBB itself."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Levodopa","desc":"Crosses BBB. Converted to dopamine in striatum. Most effective Parkinson's therapy."},{"label":"IV dopamine","desc":"Does not cross BBB. Used for heart failure/shock (peripheral effect on heart and vessels), NOT for Parkinson's."},{"label":"Dopamine agonists","desc":"Pramipexole, ropinirole — directly stimulate dopamine receptors in striatum, do cross BBB. Alternative to levodopa."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No acute indication — chronic Parkinson's treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Levodopa + carbidopa","detail":"Standard dose: 3x/day. Carbidopa 25 mg + levodopa 100 mg (Sinemet). Titrate to symptom control."},{"naam":"Wearing off","detail":"After years: levodopa effect lasts shorter → "wearing off" phenomena. Solution: higher frequency, add COMT inhibitor (entacapone) or MAO-B inhibitor."}]},{"_i":88,"q":"28-year-old woman: headache, fever 39°C, petechiae on arms and legs, neck stiffness. Diagnosis?","a":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Meningococcal meningitis/sepsis","Migraine","Herpes encephalitis"],"ex":"Meningococcal disease: meningitis + sepsis due to Neisseria meningitidis. Petechiae/purpura = vasculitis → impending sepsis. EMERGENCY: IV benzylpenicillin or cefotaxime IMMEDIATELY. Every minute counts. Isolation and prophylaxis for contacts.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Meningococcal disease is a medical emergency. Neisseria meningitidis can cause both meningitis and fulminant sepsis — sometimes both at once. The course can progress from fever to septic shock within hours. Petechiae are the alarm sign: press on the skin — if they do not blanch, it is purpura due to vasculitis.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":89,"q":"Which antibiotic works by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis?","a":["Amoxicillin","Ciprofloxacin","Doxycycline","Azithromycin"],"ex":"Beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins) inhibit the transpeptidases (PBPs) that form peptidoglycan crosslinks in the bacterial cell wall. Bacteria swell and lyse. Resistance: beta-lactamase production or PBP mutations.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":90,"q":"Which medication do you give in opioid overdose with unconsciousness and respiratory depression?","a":["Flumazenil","Naloxone","Atropine","Physostigmine"],"ex":"Naloxone: competitive opioid antagonist at the μ receptor. Rapidly reverses respiratory depression. Works shorter than opioids → repeat dosing or infusion needed. Flumazenil is the antidote for benzodiazepines. Caveat: in opioid dependence → acute withdrawal.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":91,"q":"Patient uses warfarin and starts fluconazole (fungal infection). What do you expect?","a":["Warfarin effect decreases","Warfarin effect increases → bleeding risk","No interaction","Fluconazole no longer works"],"ex":"Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9, the enzyme that breaks down warfarin → warfarin accumulates → INR rises → bleeding risk. Always check INR when adding or stopping CYP inhibitors/inducers. Other CYP2C9 inhibitors: amiodarone, metronidazole.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":92,"q":"Paracetamol overdose causes liver damage through accumulation of the toxic metabolite NAPQI.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Normally: NAPQI rapidly neutralized by glutathione. Overdose: glutathione depleted → NAPQI binds liver cells → necrosis. Antidote: N-acetylcysteine (NAC) restores glutathione. Symptoms begin only after 24-72 h — do not give NAC too late.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":93,"q":"Which diuretic inhibits the Na/K/2Cl cotransporter in the loop of Henle?","a":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Amiloride"],"ex":"Loop diuretics (furosemide): inhibit NKCC2 in the ascending limb → powerful natriuresis and diuresis. Adverse effects: hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hypomagnesemia, ototoxicity (high dose). Indications: acute pulmonary edema, heart failure, hypercalcemia.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":94,"q":"Man takes too much of his sleeping medication (benzodiazepine). He is sleepy but breathing well. Which antidote is available?","a":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","N-acetylcysteine","Atropine"],"ex":"Flumazenil: competitive GABA-A antagonist → reverses benzodiazepine effects. Short duration (30-60 min) → repeat dosing needed. Caveat: in chronic use → withdrawal seizures. Naloxone is the antidote for opioids, not benzodiazepines.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":95,"q":"Beta-1-selective beta-blockers (e.g. metoprolol) are completely safe in asthma.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Beta-1 selectivity is relative and dose-dependent. At higher doses also beta-2 blockade → bronchospasm possible. In severe asthma: avoid beta-blockers. In COPD or mild asthma: cautious use may be possible, but monitor carefully.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":96,"q":"Which medication increases the risk of rhabdomyolysis when combined with a statin?","a":["Amoxicillin","Omeprazole","Ciclosporin","Metformin"],"ex":"Ciclosporin inhibits CYP3A4 and OATP1B1 transporters → statin concentrations rise → muscle cell toxicity (rhabdomyolysis). Other interactions: fibrates + statin = increased rhabdomyolysis risk. Symptoms: muscle pain, weakness, brown urine, high CK.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":97,"q":"What mechanism does morphine have in pain control?","a":["Inhibits COX-1 and COX-2","Binds to μ-opioid receptor and reduces pain signal conduction","Blocks NMDA receptor","Increases serotonin in synapse"],"ex":"Morphine binds μ-(mu-)opioid receptors in spinal cord and brain → reduced pain signal conduction + pain perception. Adverse effects: constipation, nausea, respiratory depression, sedation. Tolerance and dependence with long-term use.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":98,"q":"Tetracyclines (doxycycline) are safe in children younger than 8 years.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Tetracyclines bind to calcium ions in bone and teeth → discoloration and enamel defects in children <8 years. Exception: serious indications such as rickettsial infections. Also avoid in pregnancy (adverse effects on fetal bone formation).","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":99,"q":"Which proton pump inhibitor (PPI) has the most clinically relevant CYP2C19 interactions?","a":["Pantoprazole","Rabeprazole","Omeprazole","Esomeprazole"],"ex":"Omeprazole is the strongest CYP2C19 inhibitor among the PPIs → can reduce the effect of clopidogrel (clopidogrel is a prodrug that needs CYP2C19 for activation). Pantoprazole has minimal CYP interactions — preferred with clopidogrel use.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":100,"q":"Heparin acts directly as an anticoagulant; vitamin K antagonists (warfarin) have a delayed effect.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Heparin activates antithrombin III → immediate inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa. Warfarin inhibits vitamin K-dependent factors (II, VII, IX, X) — but existing factors must first be broken down → full effect only after 3-5 days.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":101,"q":"Patient with gout has an acute attack. Which medication is first choice for the acute attack?","a":["Allopurinol","Colchicine or NSAID","Probenecid","Furosemide"],"ex":"Acute gout: NSAID (naproxen/indomethacin) or colchicine for analgesia and anti-inflammatory effect. Allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibitor) for PREVENTION of attacks — never start during an acute attack, worsens acute episode. Corticosteroids as alternative.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":102,"q":"Which antibiotic must NOT be combined with alcohol because of a severe disulfiram-like reaction?","a":["Amoxicillin","Metronidazole","Doxycycline","Azithromycin"],"ex":"Metronidazole inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase → with alcohol use: acetaldehyde accumulation → flushing, nausea, tachycardia, hypotension. Same reaction with tinidazole. Warn patient: no alcohol during and 48 hours after course.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":103,"q":"SSRIs (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors) work immediately: improvement of depression within 1-3 days.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. SSRIs improve depressive symptoms only after 2-4 weeks. The synaptic serotonin increase is immediate, but the therapeutic effect requires neuroplastic adaptations. Adverse effects (nausea, anxiety) can occur earlier. Continuing is important.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":104,"q":"A patient with a convulsion in the ambulance. Which medication do you give IV as the first step?","a":["Phenytoin","Valproate","Lorazepam or diazepam","Levetiracetam"],"ex":"Status epilepticus: benzodiazepines (lorazepam IV, or diazepam rectal) are the first step — rapid acting, enhance GABA signaling. If seizure does not stop after 5 min: second agent (levetiracetam, valproate, phenytoin). After 30 min: consider anesthesia.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":105,"q":"24-year-old woman: pain on urination, frequency, no fever. Urine: leukocyturia, nitrite positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Pyelonephritis","Uncomplicated urinary tract infection","Chlamydia infection","Interstitial cystitis"],"ex":"Uncomplicated UTI (cystitis): dysuria + frequency + leukocyturia + nitrite (gram-negative bacteria). No fever/flank pain → no pyelonephritis. Treatment: nitrofurantoin 5 d or fosfomycin 1 d. Not: unnecessary ciprofloxacin (resistance!). ","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":106,"q":"MRSA stands for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus and is resistant to all beta-lactam antibiotics.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. MRSA has the mecA gene → altered PBP2a → beta-lactams no longer bind. Thus resistant to penicillins, cephalosporins and carbapenems. Treatment: vancomycin or linezolid. Isolation measures required in hospital.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":107,"q":"35-year-old man after dental treatment: fever 39°C, new heart murmur, hematuria. Cardiac echo: vegetation on mitral valve. Diagnosis?","a":["Rheumatic fever","Infective endocarditis","Myocarditis","Pericarditis"],"ex":"Endocarditis: bacterial vegetations on heart valves. Duke criteria: fever + vegetation on echo + bacteremia. Causes: Streptococcus viridans (dental treatment), Staphylococcus aureus (IV drug users). Treatment: prolonged IV antibiotics, sometimes surgery.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Infective endocarditis (IE) is a bacterial (rarely fungal) infection of heart valves or endocardium. Vegetations = colonies of bacteria + fibrin + platelets on the valve surface.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Bacteremia (dentist, IV drugs, catheter)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"adherence to damaged endocardium."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"vegetation formation. Fragments detach."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"septic emboli."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"abscesses in brain, spleen, kidneys. Duke criteria: 2 major (positive cultures + echo vegetation), or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor = definite IE."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Rheumatic fever","desc":"also post-streptococcal but no vegetations, does have migratory polyarthritis + erythema marginatum."},{"label":"Myocarditis","desc":"diffuse muscle damage, high troponin, no heart murmur as main finding."},{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"friction rub, saddle-shaped ECG, no bacteremia."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"4-6 weeks IV antibiotics: penicillin G/amoxicillin (viridans streptococci), flucloxacillin (S. aureus), vancomycin (MRSA).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Surgery for","detail":"acute heart failure, persistent bacteremia, vegetation >10 mm, abscess."},{"naam":"Dental prophylaxis","detail":"amoxicillin 2 g once in high-risk patients (prosthetic valve, prior IE)."}]},{"_i":108,"q":"5-year-old child: sore throat, fever, red throat with exudate, painful cervical lymph nodes. Rapid streptococcal test positive. Treatment?","a":["Wait and see, resolves spontaneously","Amoxicillin 10 days","Ciprofloxacin 5 days","Azithromycin 1 day"],"ex":"Group A streptococcal pharyngitis: amoxicillin 10 days (penicillin V alternative). Goal: prevent rheumatic fever (heart/joint complication). In penicillin allergy: erythromycin/azithromycin. Not: antibiotics for viral pharyngitis (50-80%).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (GABHS, S. pyogenes) is the only bacterial sore throat in which antibiotic treatment is useful. The goal is not symptom shortening (1-2 days) but preventing rheumatic fever — a molecular mimicry complication that damages heart valves.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"S. pyogenes."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"post-infectious immune response."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"molecular mimicry (antibodies cross-react with heart valve and joint tissue)."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"rheumatic fever (3-4% without antibiotics). Centor score: exudate + fever + lymphadenopathy + no cough = 4 points."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"high bacterial probability."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Viral pharyngitis (50-80%!)","desc":"cough, runny nose, hoarseness present → no antibiotics."},{"label":"EBV","desc":"splenomegaly, monospot positive → no amoxicillin (rash!)."},{"label":"Peritonsillar abscess","desc":"uvula deviation, hot potato voice, unilateral swelling → urgent incision."},{"label":"Epiglottitis","desc":"severe dysphagia, drooling, stridor → ICU."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Amoxicillin 500 mg bid for 10 days (children: 40 mg/kg/day).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Penicillin V is an alternative."},{"naam":"In penicillin allergy","detail":"azithromycin 5 days or clindamycin."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Rapid strep test or throat culture before starting — do not treat blindly."}]},{"_i":109,"q":"Influenza is caused by a DNA virus.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Influenza is an RNA virus (orthomyxovirus). High mutation rate due to RNA polymerase without proofreading → antigenic drift (small annual mutations) and shift (reassortment of segments → pandemic). Vaccine updated annually.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":110,"q":"Patient with HIV, CD4 50 cells/μL: cough, fever, night sweats. Chest X-ray: bilateral perihilar infiltrates. Diagnosis?","a":["Bacterial pneumonia","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)","Tuberculosis","Lung cancer"],"ex":"PCP: most common opportunistic infection in HIV with CD4 <200. Key features: bilateral interstitial infiltrates, high LDH, subacute course. Treatment: high-dose co-trimoxazole. Prophylaxis at CD4 <200. LDH elevation is characteristic.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus that causes no problem in healthy people — the immune system clears it. But with severe immunosuppression (HIV with CD4 <200, transplant patients, high-dose steroids), it can cause life-threatening pneumonia.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":111,"q":"52-year-old man returning from Africa: fever with periods of chills, enlarged spleen. Blood smear: intraerythrocytic parasite. Diagnosis?","a":["Dengue","Malaria","Typhoid","Leptospirosis"],"ex":"Malaria: Plasmodium infection transmitted by Anopheles mosquito. Blood smear is the gold standard. P. falciparum = most dangerous (cerebral malaria). Treatment: artemisinin combination therapy. Prophylaxis: depends on destination (atovaquone/proguanil, mefloquine).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Malaria is caused by five Plasmodium species. P. falciparum: >90% of malaria mortality, no typical cycle, chloroquine-resistant in sub-Saharan Africa, can cause cerebral malaria.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Sporozoites via mosquito bite."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"liver phase (asymptomatic)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"merozoites infect red blood cells."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"cyclic rupture."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"fever. P. falciparum: infected cells are sticky."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Dengue","desc":"also tropical + fever but no parasite, does have rash + low platelets + muscle/joint pain."},{"label":"Typhoid","desc":"persistent non-cyclic fever, bradycardia, positive blood/stool culture."},{"label":"Leptospirosis","desc":"water exposure, conjunctival suffusion."},{"label":"Rickettsiae","desc":"tick bite, rash."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"P. falciparum: artemisinin combination therapy (artemether-lumefantrine).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Severe malaria","detail":"IV artesunate."},{"naam":"P. vivax/ovale","detail":"chloroquine + primaquine (eliminate liver forms — caveat G6PD deficiency!)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Choice of prophylaxis depends on country and resistance pattern."}]},{"_i":112,"q":"Vancomycin is the drug of choice in severe Clostridioides difficile infection.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Fidaxomicin is now first choice in severe/recurrent C. difficile. Oral vancomycin is an alternative for severe cases. Metronidazole only in mild infection if fidaxomicin/vancomycin not available. NO IV metronidazole anymore as monotherapy.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":113,"q":"Nursing home: 3 patients with sudden watery diarrhea after antibiotic course. Stool toxin test positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Norovirus outbreak","Clostridioides difficile infection","Salmonellosis","EHEC infection"],"ex":"C. difficile: after antibiotics (clindamycin, cephalosporins, quinolones) dysbiosis → C. diff overgrowth → toxins A+B → colonic damage. Diagnostics: toxin test or PCR. Isolation, contact precautions, stop triggering antibiotics.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":114,"q":"28-year-old woman: fever, neck stiffness, photophobia, positive Kernig.\nLP: cloudy fluid, PMN pleocytosis\nGlucose 1.8 mmol/L\nProtein 3.5 g/L\nCause?","a":["Viral meningitis","Bacterial meningitis","Cryptococcal meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis"],"ex":"Bacterial meningitis: PMN pleocytosis + low glucose (<2.5 or glucose ratio <0.5) + high protein + cloudy fluid. Causes: N. meningitidis (young people), S. pneumoniae (older adults). EMERGENCY: cefotaxime/ceftriaxone + dexamethasone. Every hour of delay = more damage.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":115,"q":"The human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine also protects against already existing HPV infections.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. HPV vaccine is prophylactic — it prevents new infections but does not treat existing infections or precancerous lesions. Therefore: vaccinate as early as possible (before sexual activity). Provides protection against HPV 16, 18 (and 6, 11 with 9-valent).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":116,"q":"42-year-old man: persistent cough >3 weeks, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis. Immunocompetent. X-ray: apical cavitations. Diagnosis?","a":["Lung cancer","Tuberculosis","Sarcoidosis","Lung abscess due to aspiration"],"ex":"TB: Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Classic: prolonged cough, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis, apical cavitations. Diagnosis: sputum culture (Ziehl-Neelsen), GeneXpert. Isolation required. Treatment: RIPE (rifampicin + isoniazid + pyrazinamide + ethambutol) 6 months.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, transmitted by droplet infection. In 90% of infections, the infection remains latent (immune system keeps it under control). Active TB develops with immunosuppression, malnutrition or high bacterial burden.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":117,"q":"Woman after vaginal delivery: fever on day 2, uterus tender, foul odor. Diagnosis?","a":["Postpartum urinary tract infection","Endometritis","Mastitis","Wound infection"],"ex":"Endometritis: uterine infection after delivery, C-section or procedure. Fever + tender uterus + foul-smelling lochia. Pathogens: mixed (E. coli, streptococci, anaerobes). Treatment: broad-spectrum antibiotics (amoxicillin-clavulanate or clindamycin + gentamicin).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":118,"q":"Lyme borreliosis is transmitted by the Ixodes tick and often presents with erythema migrans.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Borrelia burgdorferi transmitted by Ixodes ricinus (sheep tick). Erythema migrans: ring-shaped expansion around tick bite, >5 cm, characteristic of early Lyme. Treatment: doxycycline 10-21 d. Late Lyme: arthritis, neuroborreliosis.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":119,"q":"STI clinic: 25-year-old man, painless ulcer on the glans penis, regional lymphadenopathy. No pus. Diagnosis?","a":["Genital herpes","Syphilis (primary syphilis)","Gonorrhea","Chlamydia"],"ex":"Primary syphilis: painless hard ulcer (chancre) due to Treponema pallidum. Secondary syphilis: rash including palms/soles. TPHA/VDRL for diagnostics. Treatment: single-dose IM benzylpenicillin. Partner notification mandatory.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":120,"q":"Immunocompromised patient (post-transplant): fever, dry cough, CT: "halo sign" around nodules. Diagnosis?","a":["PCP","Invasive aspergillosis","CMV pneumonia","Nocardiosis"],"ex":"Invasive aspergillosis: Aspergillus fumigatus infects lungs in severely immunocompromised patients (neutropenia, steroids). CT: halo sign (hemorrhage around nodule). Diagnostics: galactomannan serum/BAL, CT, biopsy. Treatment: voriconazole.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":121,"q":"In sepsis, starting antibiotics is the highest priority — this must never wait for blood cultures.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Blood cultures must be obtained BEFORE starting antibiotics (2 sets from 2 sites) — but this should take at most a few minutes. Antibiotics must be given within 1 hour in septic shock. Priority: quickly obtain blood cultures, then IMMEDIATELY antibiotics.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":122,"q":"70-year-old patient, nursing home: fever, confusion, hematuria. Urine: pyuria, gram-negative rods on culture. Diagnosis?","a":["Uncomplicated cystitis","Urosepsis","Prostatitis","Vaginitis"],"ex":"Urosepsis: UTI with systemic signs (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, confusion). Older adults present atypically (confusion without fever). Pathogen: E. coli (80%). Treatment: IV broad-spectrum antibiotics (cefuroxime, piperacillin-tazobactam), blood cultures first.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":123,"q":"Antiretroviral therapy (ART) for HIV fully cures the infection.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. ART suppresses the HIV virus to an undetectable level but does not cure the infection — the virus remains latent in reservoir cells. Benefits: normal life expectancy, no longer transmits with viral suppression (U=U). Lifelong use required.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":124,"q":"55-year-old man:\nNa 128 mmol/L, osmolality low\nUrine Na >20 mmol/L\nNo edema — TSH and cortisol normal\nDiagnosis?","a":["Psychogenic polydipsia","SIADH","Adrenal insufficiency","Hypothyroidism"],"ex":"SIADH: inappropriate ADH secretion → water retention → dilutional hyponatremia. Key features: euvolemic + low osmolality + urine Na >20 + urine osmolality >serum osmolality. Causes: malignancy (small-cell lung carcinoma!), SSRIs, carbamazepine. Treatment: fluid restriction.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"SIADH means the body produces too much ADH (or produces it ectopically), causing it to retain too much water. The blood is effectively diluted → sodium falls. Characteristically, the patient is not edematous (euvolemic) — there is no salt deficit, only water excess.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":125,"q":"An elevated D-dimer proves that a pulmonary embolism is present.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific — elevated in any condition with fibrin breakdown (infection, trauma, pregnancy, malignancy). A normal D-dimer with low clinical probability rules out PE. Positive D-dimer: CT angiography always needed.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":126,"q":"Hyperkalemia:\nK+ 6.8 mmol/L\nECG: tented T waves, widened QRS\nMost urgent treatment?","a":["IV calcium gluconate","IV insulin + glucose","IV sodium bicarbonate","IV furosemide"],"ex":"Life-threatening hyperkalemia with ECG changes: FIRST IV calcium gluconate → stabilizes myocardial membrane (works in 1-3 min). Then: insulin+glucose (K+ into cells), salbutamol, bicarbonate, Kayexalate/dialysis for definitive K+ lowering.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":127,"q":"35-year-old woman:\nCa 3.1 mmol/L\nPTH elevated\nPhosphate low\nKidney stones\nDiagnosis?","a":["Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Vitamin D intoxication","Sarcoidosis"],"ex":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: autonomous PTH secretion (adenoma 80%). PTH ↑ + Ca ↑ = primary HPT. In malignancy: PTH low or normal. Symptoms: kidney stones, bone pain, constipation, depression ("stones, bones, groans, psychic moans"). Treatment: parathyroidectomy.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":128,"q":"A low MCV (microcytic) in anemia always indicates iron deficiency.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Microcytic anemia: iron deficiency (most common), thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease (sometimes), sideroblastic anemia. Distinguish by: ferritin (low in Fe deficiency, high in chronic disease), blood smear, Hb electrophoresis if thalassemia suspected.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":129,"q":"48-year-old man: fasting glucose value 7.2 mmol/L measured twice. Diagnosis?","a":["Normal blood sugar","Impaired fasting glycemia","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Borderline prediabetes"],"ex":"DM2 diagnosis: fasting glucose ≥7.0 mmol/L on 2 separate occasions, OR HbA1c ≥48 mmol/mol (6.5%), OR 2 h glucose tolerance test ≥11.1, OR random glucose ≥11.1 + symptoms. Here: 7.2 ≥ 7.0 = diabetes.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus is a combination of insulin resistance (cells respond less to insulin) and relative deficiency of insulin production. Glucose remains high in the blood because it cannot enter the cells. It begins insidiously, often unnoticed for years.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null}]}
